oop in find involves: accessing parent[root], checking if it equals root, XORing parity, updating root. That's about 4-5 bytecode operations per iteration. So total bytecode operations: ~500M-700M.\n\nCPython can do about 50-100M simple operations per second. So this would take 5-15 seconds. That's borderline.\n\nLet me try to optimize:\n1. Use local variable references for parent and parity arrays.\n2. Avoid function calls in the inner loop.\n\nActually, let me just write it and see. The problem likely expects this approach (since it's the standard solution for dynamic connectivity with bipartiteness queries).\n\nLet me write the final code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()\n    idx = 0\n    n = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    q = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    \n    ops = [None] * q\n    active_edges = {}\n    edge_intervals = []\n    \n    for t in range(q):\n        op = data[idx]; idx += 1\n        u = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n        v = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n        \n        if op == b'+':\n            if u > v:\n                u, v = v, u\n            active_edges[(u, v)] = t\n        elif op == b'-':\n            if u > v:\n                u, v = v, u\n            start = active_edges.pop((u, v))\n            edge_intervals.append((u, v, start, t))\n        else:\n            ops[t] = (u, v)\n    \n    for (u, v), start in active_edges.items():\n        edge_intervals.append((u, v, start, q - 1))\n    \n    # Build segment tree\n    segtree = [[] for _ in range(4 * q)]\n    \n    def add_edge(node, l, r, ql, qr, u, v):\n        if ql <= l and r <= qr:\n            segtree[node].append((u, v))\n            return\n        mid = (l + r) >> 1\n        if ql <= mid:\n            add_edge(node << 1, l, mid, ql, qr, u, v)\n        if qr > mid:\n            add_edge(node << 1 | 1, mid + 1, r, ql, qr, u, v)\n    \n    for u, v, s, e in edge_intervals:\n        if s <= e:\n            add_edge(1, 0, q - 1, s, e, u, v)\n    \n    # Rollback DSU with parity\n    parent = list(range(n + 1))\n    rank = [0] * (n + 1)\n    parity = [0] * (n + 1)\n    is_bip = [True] * (n + 1)\n    history = []\n    \n    # For faster find, use local references\n    def find(x):\n        par = parent\n        par_ = parity\n        root = x\n        p = 0\n        while par[root] != root:\n            p ^= par_[root]\n            root = par[root]\n        return root, p\n    \n    def union(u, v):\n        par = parent\n        par_ = parity\n        rnk = rank\n        bip = is_bip\n        hist = history\n        \n        # find u\n        ru = u\n        pu = 0\n        while par[ru] != ru:\n            pu ^= par_[ru]\n            ru = par[ru]\n        \n        # find v\n        rv = v\n        pv = 0\n        while par[rv] != rv:\n            pv ^= par_[rv]\n            rv = par[rv]\n        \n        if ru == rv:\n            if pu ^ pv != 1:\n                hist.append((ru, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0))\n                bip[ru] = False\n            else:\n                hist.append((-1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0))\n        else:\n            if rnk[ru] > rnk[rv]:\n                ru, rv = rv, ru\n                pu, pv = pv, pu\n            \n            hist.append((ru, 1, rv, par[ru], rnk[rv], par_[ru], bip[ru], bip[rv]))\n            \n            par[ru] = rv\n            par_[ru] = pu ^ pv ^ 1\n            bip[rv] = bip[ru] and bip[rv]\n            if rnk[ru] == rnk[rv]:\n                rnk[rv] += 1\n    \n    def rollback():\n        state = history.pop()\n        par = parent\n        par_ = parity\n        rnk = rank\n        bip = is_bip\n        \n        if state[1] == 0:\n            if state[0] != -1:\n                bip[state[0]] = True\n        else:\n            ru, _, rv, old_par, old_rnk, old_par_, old_bip_ru, old_bip_rv = state\n            par[ru] = old_par\n            rnk[rv] = old_rnk\n            par_[ru] = old_par_\n            bip[ru] = old_bip_ru\n            bip[rv] = old_bip_rv\n    \n    # Iterative DFS on segment tree\n    # Stack: (node, l, r, state) where state=0 means entering, state=1 means between children, state=2 means exiting\n    # Actually, let me use a different approach:\n    # Process nodes in order, tracking snapshots\n    \n    # Actually, recursive DFS with depth O(log q) should be fine\n    sys.setrecursionlimit(1000000)\n    \n    ans = []\n    \n    def dfs(node, l, r):\n        snapshot = len(history)\n        \n        for u, v in segtree[node]:\n            union(u, v)\n        \n        if l == r:\n            if ops[l] is not None:\n                u, v = ops[l]\n                par = parent\n                par_ = parity\n                bip = is_bip\n                \n                # find u\n                ru = u\n                pu = 0\n                while par[ru] != ru:\n                    pu ^= par_[ru]\n                    ru = par[ru]\n                \n                # find v\n                rv = v\n                pv = 0\n                while par[rv] != rv:\n                    pv ^= par_[rv]\n                    rv = par[rv]\n                \n                if ru != rv:\n                    ans.append(\"DISCONNECTED\")\n                elif not bip[ru]:\n                    ans.append(\"CONTRADICTION\")\n                elif pu == pv:\n                    ans.append(\"SAME\")\n                else:\n                    ans.append(\"DIFFERENT\")\n        else:\n            mid = (l + r) >> 1\n            dfs(node << 1, l, mid)\n            dfs(node << 1 | 1, mid + 1, r)\n        \n        while len(history) > snapshot:\n            rollback()\n    \n    dfs(1, 0, q - 1)\n    \n    sys.stdout.write('\\n'.join(ans) + '\\n')\n\nmain()\n```\n\nWait, I'm concerned about performance. Let me think about whether there's a way to reduce the constant factor.\n\nOne issue: the `union` function is called for each edge in the segment tree. The total number of such calls is O(q log q). Each call does two finds (each O(log n)) and some bookkeeping. The rollback is also O(1) per call.\n\nThe main bottleneck is the finds. Let me see if I can make them faster.\n\nIn the find loop:\n```python\nwhile par[ru] != ru:\n    pu ^= par_[ru]\n    ru = par[ru]\n```\n\nThis is already quite tight. Each iteration does 2 list accesses, 1 XOR, 1 assignment. Not much room for optimization.\n\nLet me think about the overall structure differently. Instead of a segment tree, what if I use a \"sweep line\" approach? No, that doesn't work for dynamic connectivity.\n\nI think the segment tree + rollback DSU is the best approach. Let me just make sure the code is correct and as efficient as possible.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the history storage. Instead of storing tuples, I can use a flat list:\n\n```python\nhistory = []  # flat list\n\ndef union(u, v):\n    # ... \n    # For \"same component, contradiction\":\n    history.append(ru)  # just store the root\n    # For \"same component, no issue\":\n    history.append(-1)\n    # For \"different components\":\n    history.append(ru)\n    history.append(rv)\n    history.append(par[ru])\n    history.append(rnk[rv])\n    history.append(par_[ru])\n    history.append(bip[ru])\n    history.append(bip[rv])\n```\n\nAnd for rollback, pop the appropriate number of elements. But this requires knowing how many elements to pop, which means I need to store a type indicator or use a different scheme.\n\nActually, using a flat list with a fixed format per entry would be faster than tuples. Let me use a format where the first element indicates the type:\n- Type 0 (no-op): [0]\n- Type 1 (contradiction): [1, ru]\n- Type 2 (merge): [2, ru, rv, old_par_ru, old_rnk_rv, old_parity_ru, old_bip_ru, old_bip_rv]\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me just use tuples and hope it's fast enough.\n\nActually, you know what, let me try a different approach to the history. I'll use a list of tuples but make the tuples as short as possible.\n\nLet me also think about whether I can avoid the segment tree entirely. \n\nAlternative approach: \"Offline dynamic connectivity\" using a different method.\n\nActually, I recall that there's a technique called \"divide and conquer on time\" which is essentially the same as the segment tree approach. The key insight is:\n- At each recursive call, we have a set of edges that are active throughout the entire time interval, and a set of edges that are active only during part of the interval.\n- We add all the \"always active\" edges to the DSU, then recurse on the two halves with the \"partially active\" edges.\n- On the way back, we rollback.\n\nThis is exactly the segment tree approach, just implemented differently. The segment tree is a clean way to organize which edges are \"always active\" at each node.\n\nI'll stick with the segment tree approach. Let me finalize the code.\n\nOne more optimization: instead of using a list of lists for the segment tree, I can use a dictionary or a flat array. But a list of lists should be fine.\n\nLet me also make sure I handle the edge case where q = 0 (though the constraints say q \u2265 1).\n\nLet me write the final version:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()\n    idx = 0\n    n = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    q = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    \n    ops = [None] * q\n    active_edges = {}\n    edge_intervals = []\n    \n    for t in range(q):\n        op = data[idx]; idx += 1\n        u = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n        v = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n        \n        if op == b'+':\n            if u > v:\n                u, v = v, u\n            active_edges[(u, v)] = t\n        elif op == b'-':\n            if u > v:\n                u, v = v, u\n            start = active_edges.pop((u, v))\n            edge_intervals.append((u, v, start, t))\n        else:\n            ops[t] = (u, v)\n    \n    for (u, v), start in active_edges.items():\n        edge_intervals.append((u, v, start, q - 1))\n    \n    if q == 0:\n        return\n    \n    # Build segment tree\n    segtree = [[] for _ in range(4 * q)]\n    \n    def add_edge(node, l, r, ql, qr, u, v):\n        if ql <= l and r <= qr:\n            segtree[node].append((u, v))\n            return\n        mid = (l + r) >> 1\n        if ql <= mid:\n            add_edge(node << 1, l, mid, ql, qr, u, v)\n        if qr > mid:\n            add_edge(node << 1 | 1, mid + 1, r, ql, qr, u, v)\n    \n    for u, v, s, e in edge_intervals:\n        if s <= e:\n            add_edge(1, 0, q - 1, s, e, u, v)\n    \n    # Rollback DSU with parity\n    parent = list(range(n + 1))\n    rank = [0] * (n + 1)\n    parity = [0] * (n + 1)\n    is_bip = [True] * (n + 1)\n    history = []\n    \n    def union(u, v):\n        par = parent\n        par_ = parity\n        rnk = rank\n        bip = is_bip\n        hist = history\n        \n        ru = u\n        pu = 0\n        while par[ru] != ru:\n            pu ^= par_[ru]\n            ru = par[ru]\n        \n        rv = v\n        pv = 0\n        while par[rv] != rv:\n            pv ^= par_[rv]\n            rv = par[rv]\n        \n        if ru == rv:\n            if (pu ^ pv) != 1:\n                hist.append((0, ru))\n                bip[ru] = False\n            else:\n                hist.append((0, -1))\n        else:\n            if rnk[ru] > rnk[rv]:\n                ru, rv = rv, ru\n                pu, pv = pv, pu\n            \n            hist.append((1, ru, rv, par[ru], rnk[rv], par_[ru], bip[ru], bip[rv]))\n            \n            par[ru] = rv\n            par_[ru] = pu ^ pv ^ 1\n            bip[rv] = bip[ru] and bip[rv]\n            if rnk[ru] == rnk[rv]:\n                rnk[rv] += 1\n    \n    def do_rollback():\n        state = history.pop()\n        par = parent\n        par_ = parity\n        rnk = rank\n        bip = is_bip\n        \n        if state[0] == 0:\n            if state[1] != -1:\n                bip[state[1]] = True\n        else:\n            _, ru, rv, op_, or_, opar_, obip_ru, obip_rv = state\n            par[ru] = op_\n            rnk[rv] = or_\n            par_[ru] = opar_\n            bip[ru] = obip_ru\n            bip[rv] = obip_rv\n    \n    ans = []\n    \n    sys.setrecursionlimit(1000000)\n    \n    def dfs(node, l, r):\n        snapshot = len(history)\n        \n        for u, v in segtree[node]:\n            union(u, v)\n        \n        if l == r:\n            qop = ops[l]\n            if qop is not None:\n                u, v = qop\n                par = parent\n                par_ = parity\n                bip = is_bip\n                \n                ru = u\n                pu = 0\n                while par[ru] != ru:\n                    pu ^= par_[ru]\n                    ru = par[ru]\n                \n                rv = v\n                pv = 0\n                while par[rv] != rv:\n                    pv ^= par_[rv]\n                    rv = par[rv]\n                \n                if ru != rv:\n                    ans.append(\"DISCONNECTED\")\n                elif not bip[ru]:\n                    ans.append(\"CONTRADICTION\")\n                elif pu == pv:\n                    ans.append(\"SAME\")\n                else:\n                    ans.append(\"DIFFERENT\")\n        else:\n            mid = (l + r) >> 1\n            dfs(node << 1, l, mid)\n            dfs(node << 1 | 1, mid + 1, r)\n        \n        while len(history) > snapshot:\n            do_rollback()\n    \n    dfs(1, 0, q - 1)\n    \n    sys.stdout.write('\\n'.join(ans) + '\\n')\n\nmain()\n```\n\nLet me trace through the sample to verify:\n\nInput:\n```\n5 13\n+ 1 2    (t=0)\n+ 2 3    (t=1)\n? 1 3    (t=2)\n+ 3 1    (t=3)\n? 1 2    (t=4)\n- 3 1    (t=5)\n? 1 3    (t=6)\n+ 4 5    (t=7)\n? 1 5    (t=8)\n+ 3 4    (t=9)\n? 1 5    (t=10)\n+ 1 5    (t=11)\n? 2 4    (t=12)\n```\n\nEdge intervals:\n- (1,2): added at t=0, never removed \u2192 [0, 12]\n- (2,3): added at t=1, never removed \u2192 [1, 12]\n- (1,3): add